  { q: 'Do ravens play?', a: 'Yes. Ravens engage in multiple forms of play: aerial play (rolling, tumbling, diving in pairs and groups), object play (manipulating items with no food value), snow sliding, and what appear to be social games with other ravens and other species. Play is well-documented in Common Ravens by Bernd Heinrich and others.' },
  { q: 'Why do ravens play?', a: 'The function of play in ravens is not fully established. Proposed functions include: practice of motor skills (aerial manoeuvrability), social bonding, exploration and cognitive development, and intrinsic reinforcement (play may be self-rewarding). Play is most common in young ravens, consistent with a developmental/learning function.' },
